BACKGROUND = [
    {"id": "bg-collective", "after": "intro-p2", "title": "Collective fields at large N", "audience": ["physicist", "student"],
     "body": r"""<p>In a theory of $N$ fields in the vector representation, every singlet observable can be written through bilinears such as $G(x_1,x_2)=\frac1N\sum_I\phi^*_I(x_1)\phi_I(x_2)$. Jevicki and Sakita showed that one can change variables in the path integral from the $\phi_I$ to these collective fields. The change of variables costs a Jacobian, $\exp[(N-V)\operatorname{Tr}\log G]$ on a lattice of $V$ points, so the whole action becomes proportional to $N$. At large $N$ the path integral is then dominated by a saddle, the free propagator for free fields, and $1/\sqrt N$ fluctuations around it reproduce the $1/N$ expansion, with $1/N$ playing the role of $\hbar$. The collective field has two points, not one, so it has more variables than a local field in $d$ dimensions. The paper's starting point is that a function of two boundary points carries exactly the content of a tower of fields of every spin in $AdS_{d+1}$.</p>"""},
    {"id": "bg-gauge-bc", "after": "intro-p3", "title": "Gauge fields in AdS and boundary conditions", "audience": ["physicist", "student"],
     "body": r"""<p>A bulk gauge field in $AdS_{d+1}$ has two possible behaviours near the boundary $z\to0$: a constant mode $a_i(x)$ and a mode falling as $z^{d-2}$, whose coefficient is a conserved current $j_i(x)$ of dimension $d-1$. The standard choice fixes $a_i$: it is a source, and the CFT has a global $U(1)$ with current $j_i$. One may instead let $a_i$ fluctuate and integrate over it. In the CFT this couples a dynamical gauge field to $j_i$, gauging the symmetry. For $d<4$ both choices give normalisable modes, and Witten showed that the $SL(2,\mathbb Z)$ action on three-dimensional CFTs with a $U(1)$ maps them into each other; adding a Chern–Simons term gives a mixed condition. These were statements about correlators; the paper derives them from the CFT, off-shell, for scalar QED.</p>"""},
    {"id": "bg-monopoles", "after": "restrict-p1", "title": "Monopole operators and the topological symmetry", "audience": ["student"],
     "body": r"""<p>In a three-dimensional $U(1)$ gauge theory the dual field strength $j^T_i=\frac1{2\pi}\varepsilon_{ijl}\partial_jA_l$ is conserved identically, so the theory has a topological $U(1)_T$ global symmetry. Operators charged under it are monopole operators: they create a unit of magnetic flux through any sphere surrounding their insertion point, much as a 't Hooft line does in four dimensions. In other dimensions the same construction gives operators of codimension three. With a Chern–Simons term at level $k$, Gauss's law ties electric charge to magnetic flux, so a monopole operator of unit flux also carries $k$ units of gauge charge. To be gauge invariant it must then be dressed with charged matter fields. In the paper that dressing is what conflicts with the way the singlet sector is defined.</p>"""},
    {"id": "bg-harmonic", "after": "sec-review-p2", "title": "Harmonic analysis on the conformal group", "audience": ["physicist", "student"],
     "body": r"""<p>Just as Fourier analysis expands functions on $\mathbb R^d$ in plane waves, harmonic analysis on the Euclidean conformal group $SO(d+1,1)$ expands conformally covariant functions in its irreducible representations. A function of two points can be decomposed into conformal three-point structures with an operator of dimension $\Delta$ and spin $J$ at a third point $y$. The complete set for normalisable functions is the principal series, $\Delta=\frac d2+is$ with real $s$, so the expansion is a sum over $J$ and an integral over $s$ and $y$. Structures at $\Delta$ and at $d-\Delta$ are related by a shadow transform, so only half are independent. Poles of the coefficients off the principal series, reached by deforming the contour, correspond to actual operators. The paper expands bi-locals and bulk fields in the same representations, which is what identifies them.</p>"""},
    {"id": "bg-ads-fields", "after": "sec-review-p5", "title": "Fields in AdS and their fall-offs", "audience": ["student"],
     "body": r"""<p>In Poincaré coordinates, $ds^2=(dx^idx^i+dz^2)/z^2$, the boundary is at $z=0$. A scalar field of mass $m$ solving the wave equation behaves near the boundary as $z^{\Delta}$ or $z^{d-\Delta}$, with $m^2=\Delta(\Delta-d)$; a field of spin $J$ behaves similarly with powers shifted by $J$. The bulk-to-boundary propagator is the solution sourced at one boundary point, falling as $z^{d-\Delta}$ times a delta function there and as $z^\Delta$ times a two-point function elsewhere. In the standard dictionary the coefficient of the slower fall-off is the source and that of the faster one the expectation value of an operator of dimension $\Delta$. For a massless spin-$J$ field dual to a conserved current, $\Delta=d-2+J$. The paper uses these propagators also as a basis for expanding bulk fields.</p>"""},
    {"id": "bg-double-trace", "after": "sec-action-p2", "title": "Double-trace deformations and alternate quantization", "audience": ["physicist", "student"],
     "body": r"""<p>When a bulk scalar has mass in the window $-\frac{d^2}{4}<m^2<-\frac{d^2}{4}+1$, both fall-offs $z^{\Delta}$ and $z^{d-\Delta}$ are normalisable, and either may be held fixed: the dual operator then has dimension $\Delta$ or $d-\Delta$. Witten, and Klebanov and Witten, showed that a double-trace deformation $f\int\mathcal O^2$ of the CFT acts in the bulk as a mixed boundary condition, and that its flow to large $f$ swaps one quantization for the other. Klebanov and Polyakov used this to relate the free $O(N)$ model, with $\phi^2$ of dimension $d-2$, to the critical one, where it is replaced by an operator of dimension 2. The bulk equations do not change; only the propagators' boundary behaviour does. The paper derives this from the CFT for the scalar, and its analogue for the vector when the $U(1)$ is gauged.</p>"""},
]
